#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
yt-gem v1.0 — AI-agent-native YouTube distiller via free Gemini web.

Problem: another AI agent "watching" a YouTube video is expensive — download,
frames, transcription, and a huge context bill, for a result that is often
just a thin summary. Free Gemini web already watches the video natively when
you paste its URL into chat. yt-gem turns that into a one-command,
machine-consumable distillation:

    yt-gem https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dQw4w9WgXcQ
        -> {"ok": true, "f": ".../dQw4w9WgXcQ.detail.md", "s": 8421,
            "video_id": "dQw4w9WgXcQ", "mode": "detail", "sections": [...], ...}

The full distillation lives on disk (markdown). Stdout is only a ~200-char
JSON pointer, so the calling agent can decide what to read — the TL;DR, one
chapter slice, or the claims list — instead of swallowing the whole video.

Prototype: lesterppo/hermes-gem-pw (gem-pw / gemini-web). yt-gem reuses its
proven patterns, not its code by copy-paste accident:
  * real headed Chromium via Playwright (Gemini's endpoints are browser-gated;
    curl/httpx/WebAPI clients are silently dropped — see gem-pw README),
  * CDP-first (warm browser on 127.0.0.1:9224) with headed fallback,
  * the SAME browser profile as gem-pw (~/.gemini-cli/cr-profile, falling
    back to the gem-pw-login profile), so one sign-in serves both tools,
  * a shared profile lock (~/.hermes/gem_pw.lock) so yt-gem and gem-pw never
    collide on the same Chromium profile,
  * xvfb-run fallback when DISPLAY is unset, locale-agnostic selectors,
  * placeholder-guard + stability polling for the response (never return a
    half-streamed "Analyzing…" as a result),
  * agent-native error codes (NOT_SIGNED_IN, BAD_URL, VIDEO_NOT_ACCESSIBLE,
    EMPTY, LOCKED, NO_INPUT, TYPE_FAILED).

Usage:
  yt-gem <youtube-url-or-id>                  Detailed distillation (default)
  yt-gem <url> --brief                        TL;DR + takeaways only
  yt-gem <url> --chapters                     Timestamped chapter breakdown only
  yt-gem <url> --claims                       Verifiable claims + evidence only
  yt-gem <url> "What does the speaker say about X?"
                                              Answer one question about the video
  yt-gem --followup -c sess.json "Follow-up?" Ask a follow-up in the same
                                              Gemini conversation (no re-watch)
  yt-gem <url> --dry-run                      Parse + build prompt, no browser
  yt-gem <url> --dry-run --print-prompt       Show the exact prompt sent
  yt-gem <url> -o out.md -t 600               Custom output, longer timeout
  yt-gem <url> --force                        Ignore the cache, re-distill
  yt-gem <url> --gem <gem-id>                 Distill through a specific Gem

Caching: a default-mode distillation is cached at
  ~/.hermes/yt_gem_output/<video_id>.<mode>.md
plus a <video_id>.<mode>.json sidecar (sections, timestamps, word count).
A second agent asking for the same video + mode gets the cached pointer in
milliseconds and Gemini is never re-asked. Custom questions and follow-ups
are never cached (they are conversation, not a canonical distillation).

Honesty rules (baked into every prompt, and into the output contract):
  * Timestamps are Gemini's, not verified against the video. Treat them as
    pointers ("look near 12:34"), not as ground truth.
  * A quote is only quoted verbatim when Gemini is confident; otherwise it
    is paraphrased. The prompt forces this distinction.
  * If Gemini cannot access/play the video (private, removed, age-gated,
    region-blocked), it must answer with the single sentinel
    VIDEO_NOT_ACCESSIBLE — yt-gem turns that into a machine error instead
    of a hallucinated summary. A hallucinated summary is worse than no
    summary; this tool refuses to produce one silently.
  * This is NOT a verbatim transcript. If you need exact wording, watch the
    captioned segment at the cited timestamp.

No API key. No cookies in code. Sign in once (gem-pw-login, or the bundled
yt-gem-login) and the profile persists.

CONNECTION FIX (2026-10-25, this VM): Chromium cannot do this VM's egress
proxy auth directly — it fails with ERR_TUNNEL_CONNECTION_FAILED, while
curl/Python through the same proxy work, and gemini.google.com additionally
drops HTTP/1.1 requests after a successful CONNECT+TLS (curl --http2
returns 200, curl default returns 000). The proven route, taken from
tos-cli/proxy_fwd.py, is a localhost CONNECT forwarder
(vendored as proxy_fwd.py): it listens on 127.0.0.1:8888 with no auth,
dials the upstream proxy at its IPv4 address explicitly (the proxy
hostname's IPv6 record hangs from this VM), and injects
Proxy-Authorization from $https_proxy. yt-gem starts that forwarder
automatically on this VM and points headed Chromium at it, with
ignore_https_errors (the TLS-intercept CA is not trusted by Playwright
Chromium for every host). Chromium negotiates HTTP/2 by default, so the
HTTP/1.1 drop does not apply to the browser path. Set
YT_GEM_NO_FWD=1 to disable the forwarder on a normal network.
"""

